<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bujang Valley</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bujang Valley is an archaeological region near Merbok in Kedah, north-western Malaysia, between Gunung Jerai and the Muda River. It contains the remains of more than fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist brick and laterite shrines, called candi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sungai-batu">Sungai Batu</a>, dating from the early centuries CE to about the 14th century. The sites show Old Kedah as an early port linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Malay world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bujang-valley.jpg" alt="Bujang Valley" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gryffindor · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muzium_Negara_KL19b.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
